Third-party material
====================

The packages contain no code, test data or other material taken from another project.

cap-dotnet is a port of cap-std (https://github.com/bytecodealliance/cap-std), which is
licensed Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT. What this repository takes from
cap-std is its design: the idea of a directory handle as the authority, and the shape of the
API built on it. That is re-expressed in C# from scratch. cap-std's test suite was used as a
checklist of cases to cover. Every test here was written for this repository, and none is a
translation of an upstream test.

The packages have no runtime dependencies beyond .NET itself, with one exception below. The
analyzer shipped in Cap.Std runs inside the compiler that loads it, and brings no Roslyn
assemblies of its own.

Runtime dependencies
====================

Cap.IO.Abstractions depends on two NuGet packages, which are not included in it and are
installed alongside it:

  - TestableIO.System.IO.Abstractions (https://github.com/TestableIO/System.IO.Abstractions),
    MIT licence;
  - Testably.Abstractions.FileSystem.Interface
    (https://github.com/Testably/Testably.Abstractions), MIT licence.

Cap.IO.Abstractions implements the interfaces they declare. No code from either is copied into
this repository. No other package depends on anything beyond .NET.

Borrowing from upstream
=======================

If code or test data is ever copied or translated from cap-std, take it under cap-std's MIT
option, which is compatible with this repository's licence. Then:

  1. keep the upstream copyright notice in each file that carries the borrowed material;
  2. add an entry below naming the upstream files, the commit they were taken from and the
     files here that derive from them;
  3. include cap-std's MIT licence text below that entry.

The same applies to any other project's material, under that project's licence.

